\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Massless fields with a mean-zero constraint}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let a nonempty finite graph be connected, with positive edge weights,
and put #{n=|V|}. Its [Laplacian](fdip-0007) #{L} has the
[constant kernel](fdip-0008) spanned by
#{u_0=n^{-1/2}\mathbf1_V}. The \newvocab{mean-zero field space} is
##{\begin{aligned}
H&=u_0^\perp\\
 &=\left\{f\in\mathbb R^V:\sum_v f(v)=0\right\}.
\end{aligned}}
The massless weight #{\exp(-\phi^TL\phi/2)} has infinite integral over
#{\mathbb R^V}, but has a finite positive integral on #{H} with its
[induced Euclidean Lebesgue measure](fdip-002M). These are different reference
measures and different field spaces.}

\proof{
\p{Symmetry and #{Lu_0=0} imply #{L(H)\subset H}, since
#{\langle u_0,Lh\rangle=\langle Lu_0,h\rangle=0}.
If #{h\in H} has zero energy, the kernel theorem makes #{h} constant;
its zero sum forces #{h=0}. Thus #{L_H=L|_H} is positive definite.
Choose an orthonormal eigenbasis #{u_1,\ldots,u_{n-1}} of #{H}, with
positive eigenvalues #{\lambda_a}. Write
#{\phi=cu_0+\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}y_au_a}. This [orthogonal change of coordinates](fdip-002L) preserves volume, and
##{\phi^TL\phi=\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}\lambda_a y_a^2.}
Tonelli applies to the nonnegative weight. The integral in #{y} is
finite and positive by [Gaussian normalization](fdip-002T), whereas
the integral of one in #{c\in\mathbb R} is infinite. The full-space
massless partition function is therefore infinite.}

\p{Define #{dh} on #{H} by its orthonormal coordinates #{y}; another
orthonormal choice preserves this measure. The corresponding normalized
law is
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_H&=\frac{(2\pi)^{(n-1)/2}}
             {\sqrt{\prod_{a=1}^{n-1}\lambda_a}},\\
d\mu_H(h)&=Z_H^{-1}\exp(-h^TLh/2)\,dh.
\end{aligned}}
For #{n=1}, the space #{H=\{0\}} has dimension zero. Its Euclidean
measure has mass one, the empty product is one, and #{Z_H=1}; hence
#{\mu_H} is the point mass at zero. This convention also makes the
preceding divergence argument valid for a single vertex.}

\p{Define an operator on the full vertex space by
##{L^+=\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}\lambda_a^{-1}u_au_a^T.}
It is zero on the constants and equals #{L_H^{-1}} on #{H}; this
characterizes it independently of the chosen eigenbasis. Orthogonality
gives
##{LL^+=L^+L=P_H=I-u_0u_0^T.}
Thus #{L^+} is the \newvocab{Laplacian pseudoinverse} in the counting
pairing. Applying [the covariance formula](fdip-000B) in the
#{n-1} Euclidean coordinates of #{H}, then expanding
#{h_v=\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(u_a)_v y_a}, gives
##{\int_H h_vh_w\,d\mu_H=(L^+)_{vw}.}
For #{n=1}, both sides are zero directly. This covariance is an operator
on the full vertex space even though the probability measure is supported
on #{H}; it is not an inverse of #{L} on the full vertex space.}
}

\p{For a [polynomial observable](fdip-0006) #{X} under the
[Gaussian law](fdip-0005), its \vocabk{variance}{fdip-002Z} is expressed using
[expectation](fdip-0006) by
##{\operatorname{Var}_Q(X)
   =\left\langle (X-\langle X\rangle_Q)^2\right\rangle_Q.}
For every source field #{j}, the vanishing coordinate means give
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle j^T\phi\rangle_Q
  &=\sum_v j_v\langle\phi_v\rangle_Q\\
  &=0.
\end{aligned}}
Thus [the covariance formula](fdip-000B) implies
##{\operatorname{Var}_{Q_m}(j^T\phi)=j^TG_mj.}}

\p{The massive [Green operator](fdip-000A) separates the constant mode:
##{\begin{aligned}
(L+m^2I)^{-1}
  &=m^{-2}u_0u_0^T\\
  &\quad+\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}
       \frac{u_au_a^T}{\lambda_a+m^2}.
\end{aligned}}
Its full covariance has no finite limit as #{m\downarrow0}: taking
#{j=u_0} gives constant-mode variance #{m^{-2}}, and each diagonal entry contains
#{1/(nm^2)}. For a mean-zero source #{j\in H}, the linear observable
#{j^T\phi} instead has
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}_{Q_m}(j^T\phi)
 &=\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}
       \frac{(u_a^Tj)^2}{\lambda_a+m^2}\\
 &\longrightarrow j^TL^+j.
\end{aligned}}
This includes field differences #{j=\delta_v-\delta_w}. The limit
describes those observables and does not normalize the divergent
full-space massless weight.}

\p{Other constraints require their own measures. Fixing #{\phi(v)=a}
uses an affine hyperplane with its induced Euclidean measure; it is a
linear subspace when #{a=0}. On a connected graph this removes the
constant freedom, but generally gives different coordinate covariances
from the mean-zero constraint. Fixed
external boundary values in finite lattice regions also remove zero
modes; this is why a massless finite-volume law can exist in
\citet{Definition 8.1, Remark 8.2 and Exercise 8.2, p. 381}{friedli2017statistical}.
The boundary condition is part of that model. If the present graph has
#{k>1} connected components, one total mean-zero constraint leaves
#{k-1} constant modes. One must remove every componentwise constant mode,
for example by imposing zero sum separately on each component.}
